From dcad4bc531cb121a6be0809e681dca86c4877fb0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: elkir0 <elkir0@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/7] arm64: exynos5433: start the secondary CPUs without PSCI

The Exynos 5433 of the Galaxy Tab S2 8.0 (SM-T715) has no PSCI: its
signed bootloader offers none, and the 32-bit kernel of this board starts
the other seven cores itself, through the PMU and the secure monitor
(arch/arm/mach-exynos/platsmp.c).  A 64-bit kernel has no machine to hang
such code on, and without a method only its boot CPU ever runs.

This adds the method, for the enable-method "samsung,exynos5433-el2",
under CONFIG_ARM64_EXYNOS5433_SMP (default n).  The CPU side is the
32-bit kernel's sequence, register for register: the entry address goes
to the monitor's boot register (sysram-ns + 0x1c), the PMU is prepared as
the vendor kernel prepares it, and a core is powered on, waited for (up
to 10 ms) and released with dsb + sev, with neither SMC nor IPI, as the
vendor kernel does.

What differs is what the core finds at the entry.  The monitor releases
it in AArch32, so a 32-bit stub there makes the monitor's call
SMC 0x82000400, which switches the core to AArch64 at EL2 with the MMU
and the caches off.  An AArch64 stub then puts EL2 in the state the
loader gives the boot CPU (RW, EL1PCTEN and EL1PCEN, CNTVOFF clear, no
trap on FP/SIMD or on debug) and erets to EL1, as that loader does, so
that every CPU starts at the same level; then a holding pen waits for the
CPU's own MPIDR before it joins secondary_entry.

The pen is needed because the monitor has one boot register for all the
cores: a core that an event meant for another one releases must not run
on with a secondary_data that is not its own.  It is the spin-table's
scheme, with the full MPIDR rather than MPIDR[3:0], which does not tell
an A53 from an A57.

The stubs run with the MMU and the caches off at their physical address:
position independent, no stack, nothing written.  The AArch32 one is
hand-assembled as words, because no AArch32 assembler builds an arm64
kernel.  The pen sits in .mmuoff.data.read, the section the kernel keeps
for data read with the MMU off, and is cleaned to the point of
coherency by the CPU that writes it.

Hot-unplug is not offered: without a cpu_die the kernel refuses to take a
CPU offline (op_cpu_disable()), and the monitor's shutdown call has not
been tried from AArch64.  After a core fails to start, no other one is
started and the PMU registers of the failed one are logged.  With "nosmp"
nothing of this runs: smp_prepare_cpus() does not call cpu_prepare().

Nothing here touches a clock, a voltage or a power domain.

Not run on the device.  Compiled for arm64; the stubs were checked in the
linked image (the words of the 32-bit entry, the 2 KiB between the two
entries, the pen in its section).
---
 arch/arm64/Kconfig                      |  16 +
 arch/arm64/kernel/Makefile              |   1 +
 arch/arm64/kernel/cpu_ops.c             |   6 +
 arch/arm64/kernel/smp_exynos5433.c      | 490 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 arch/arm64/kernel/smp_exynos5433_head.S | 150 ++++++++
 5 files changed, 663 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 arch/arm64/kernel/smp_exynos5433.c
 create mode 100644 arch/arm64/kernel/smp_exynos5433_head.S

diff --git a/arch/arm64/Kconfig b/arch/arm64/Kconfig
index 7ae3cd7cc..e7cb92fa0 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/Kconfig
+++ b/arch/arm64/Kconfig
@@ -2431,6 +2431,22 @@ config ARM64_ACPI_PARKING_PROTOCOL
 	  protocol even if the corresponding data is present in the ACPI
 	  MADT table.
 
+config ARM64_EXYNOS5433_SMP
+	bool "Exynos 5433: start secondary CPUs without PSCI"
+	depends on ARCH_EXYNOS
+	help
+	  On a device whose signed bootloader offers no PSCI (Samsung Galaxy
+	  Tab S2 8.0 LTE, SM-T715), the Exynos 5433 starts its secondary CPUs
+	  the way the vendor's 32-bit kernel does: the PMU powers a core on,
+	  the secure monitor releases it in AArch32 at an address the kernel
+	  leaves in one of its registers, and an SMC switches it to AArch64 at
+	  EL2.
+
+	  Say Y to have the secondary CPUs of such a device with a 64-bit
+	  kernel. It is used by the CPUs whose device tree node says
+	  enable-method = "samsung,exynos5433-el2". With "nosmp", or on a tree
+	  without that property, nothing of it runs.
+
 config CMDLINE
 	string "Default kernel command string"
 	default ""
